parser sketch: Parse.name/binding for the command/tool name; the
clauses as parenthesized keyword blocks (Parse.$$$ inside Args.parens),
each optional, any order, at most once. params entries:
  Parse.binding -- ($$$ "::" |-- type) -- Scan.option ($$$ "=" |-- value)
  -- Parse.document_source (the description cartouche)
type = string|nat|int|bool|term|typ|fact|enum(...)|list-of-scalar.

semantics per form
------------------

- diag form (mcp_tool CMD ...): registration-time checks —
  Outer_Syntax.check_command CMD (reports the position -> ctrl+click
  to the command), Keyword.is_diag or error naming the command's
  actual keyword class; format placeholders resolve to declared
  params, all required params used. default description = the
  command's outer-syntax comment (Keyword.command_spec? verify the
  accessor at implementation; else require an explicit description
  and note it). default params/format (none given) = input :: string,
  "CMD $input".
- run function built at declaration: parse+validate the named args
  (Value.parse_* per type; term/typ/fact elaborated against the run
  context — errors name the param), substitute into the format with
  TYPE-DIRECTED QUOTING (string -> inner-string quoted+escaped,
  term/typ -> cartouche, nat/int/bool -> literal, list -> joined),
  assemble "CMD args", parse at Position.line 1 (spike 2: NOT
  Position.none, which erases positions), execute against
  Toplevel.make_state of the target context (default: registering
  theory; {repl: R} param when the repl plumbing of tool_scope
  exists), capture via the MCP_Repl output-routing buffers (they
  exist; their two unchecked ml-unit boxes in the phase-2 testing
  list get closed as part of this plan's step 1).
- position normalization: assembled text puts the command + open
  cartouche on line 1, snippet lines start at line 2; a reported
  "line N" rewrites to "line N-1 of your input" in the returned
  error text.
- string_fun (= \<open>ML\<close>): the plain string -> string form
  (the mvp tool shape), schema {input}. registration goes through
  MCP_Tool.declare like every other form — there is no separate
  register api (decided 2026-07-11, plans/mcp_tool_registry).
- ml_run (= run \<open>...\<close> (params ...)): full-power hatch,
  Proof.context -> (string*string) list -> string with declared
  params; the combinators make this the wrapper for existing
  community ML tools.

MCP_Combinators (same theory, under the command)
-------------------------------------------------

  param : {name, typ, required, default, descr} -> param
  quote_string / quote_term / quote_typ / join_list / flag
  diag  : string -> param list -> ((string*string) list -> string)
          -> tool      (* what the command's diag form compiles to *)
  func  : (string -> string) -> tool
  ml_run: (Proof.context -> (string*string) list -> string)
          -> param list -> tool

the command is a thin parser over these; ml-unit tests target the
combinators directly, the command tests only the parsing/validation.

schema over the bridge
----------------------

registry rows serialize params as yxml tuples (name, type, required,
default?, description, enum items); scala expands to json schema at
the tools/list merge: string/integer/boolean, enum lists, list-of ->
array items, defaults + descriptions carried; term/typ/fact -> string
with the validation contract stated in the property description.
form tag -> annotations only (diag_wrap: readOnly+idempotent;
string_fun: readOnly unknown -> omit hints; method_wrap: mutating).

first users (registration test theory + acceptance)
----------------------------------------------------

re-derive in isar, in a test theory: find_theorems (params criteria/
limit), ML_val (params src :: string; the position-normalization
case), value (params t :: term). these three cover the quoting
matrix (string, term, nat) and the error paths.

implementation steps
--------------------

1. close the phase-2 output-routing ml-unit boxes (writeln lands in
   the registered buffer only; transparent when idle) — the capture
   substrate this plan builds on.
2. MCP_Combinators + the assemble/quote/validate pipeline as pure ML
   (no outer syntax yet). [ml unit: quoting matrix, arg validation,
   typed errors]
3. exec-at-position runner + capture + normalization. [ml unit:
   find_consts through the runner returns output; ML_val error
   reports snippet-relative line — spikes 1-2 made durable]
4. the mcp_tool command: parser, registration-time validation,
   compilation onto the combinators; mcp_resource command (bare-name
   / isar / ML forms per the phase-2 section, now over the pivoted
   MCP_Resource). [ml unit: registration cases incl. rejection of
   non-diag commands and unknown params]
5. tools_changed emit verification end to end (the emit itself ships
   with plans/mcp_tool_registry's declare): scala forwards
   notifications/tools/list_changed; initialize declares
   tools: {listChanged: true} — closes the last open half of the
   phase-2 builtin-table item. [scala unit + bridge + e2e]
6. schema expansion scala-side + Fake_Backend rows with params.
   [scala unit]
7. e2e: the registration test theory served over stdio; tools/call
   find_theorems (isar-derived) returns conjI for "conjI"; ML_val
   tool returns a line-normalized error; list_changed notification
   observed on load_theory of the registering theory.

ASSUMPTIONS -> tests
---------------------

- diag execution + capture works in the serving process, not only in
  ML_process [bridge: run a wrapped find_consts through MCP.run_tool
  in the live session]
- build-time registration self-tests are safe (command-in-command:
  the runner executes a diag command while the mcp_tool command
  itself runs, if we validate by dry-running — DECISION: we do NOT
  dry-run at registration; only mcp_test (its own plan) executes
  tools at build time, so this plan takes no command-in-command risk
  beyond what spike 1 proved]
- keyword comment as default description is accessible [ml unit;
  if the accessor doesn't exist, the fallback (mandatory description)
  is specced above — either way a test pins the chosen behavior]
- quoting injection: a string param containing cartouche/quote
  characters cannot escape its quoting context [ml unit: adversarial
  values round-trip as data, never parse as isar]
